osted some chips. My opponent limped in, which often signals a weak hand, so I should be aggressive. The main decision is how much to raise. A min-raise is an option, but I want to build the pot and take control.  \n\n**Evaluation:**\n\nChecking is safe, but it gives away the initiative. Raising is the standard play with AK offsuit, especially against a limper. Raising to 200 is a min-raise but I want to raise bigger. All-in is very aggressive.\n\n**Reason:**\n\nRaising with Ace-King puts pressure on the limper, builds the pot with a strong hand, and helps me take control of the hand. I have a premium hand, so a bigger raise is preferred.\n\n**Answer:** 400\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: Your hand, King of Hearts and Ace of Clubs (AKo), is a premium starting hand with significant equity against a random range. The opponent has limped (called the big blind), which often signals a weak or mediocre hand, or occasionally a trap. Checking allows you to see a free flop but fails to build the pot or capitalize on your hand strength. Raising is the superior play to isolate the opponent, take the initiative, and extract value while your hand range is uncapped.\n\nReason: Raising to 4 times the big blind puts pressure on the opponent and punishes the passive limp. With AKo, you want to build a pot early and define the opponent's range.\n\nAnswer: 400"}
