ens) on a board of K, 8, 9, J, 10. The board is very coordinated, containing four cards to a straight (8-9-10-J). This means any Queen or Seven in the opponent's hand completes a straight, which would beat my Two Pair. Additionally, sets or better two pairs are possible, though less likely given the action.\n\nThe opponent has played passively (Check-Call Flop, Check Turn, Check River). This line often indicates a marginal hand (like a weak King or middle pair) or a missed draw, but checking the river could also be a trap with a straight.\n\nIf I bet, a worse hand (like K2 or 98) will likely fold due to the scary board texture (4-straight). A better hand (Straight, Set) will call or raise. Thus, betting has little value as it folds out what I beat and builds a pot against what beats me.\nSince I am down significantly in cumulative chips, I need to preserve my stack and take wins where possible without reckless gambling. Checking guarantees I realize my equity at showdown. If my hand is good, I win the pot. If not, I lose nothing extra.\n\nReason: The board (8-9-10-J-K) has a 4-card straight sequence (8-9-10-J), making any Queen or 7 a winning straight. My Two Pair is a strong showdown hand but effectively a bluff-catcher here. Betting serves no purpose as worse hands fold and better hands call. Checking is the optimal play to win the pot if ahead or minimize loss if behind.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
